`lib/bcachefs/src/engine` reads upstream's on-disk format, and the only honest
judge of a format reader is bytes the format's own implementation wrote. This
fixture is those bytes. It was produced by `bcachefs-tools` v1.39.4 running
in a QEMU guest booted from the Arch Linux 2026.09.01 install medium, as a
development instrument: neither the tools nor that medium is a dependency of
this repository, nothing fetches either, and the suite reads only what is
committed here.

The commands, in the guest, against a 32 MiB raw disk on `/dev/vdc`:

    mkdir -p /tmp/src/Documents/deep /tmp/src/empty
    printf hello-toyos > /tmp/src/Documents/a.txt
    seq 1 40000       > /tmp/src/Documents/deep/seq.txt
    ln -s ../a.txt      /tmp/src/Documents/deep/link
    bcachefs format --block_size=4096 --fs_label=toyosfix /dev/vdc
    bcachefs fusemount /dev/vdc /mnt/vdc
    cp -a /tmp/src/. /mnt/vdc/ && sync
    fusermount3 -u /mnt/vdc
    bcachefs fsck -y /dev/vdc

`--block_size=4096` is explicit because `bcachefs format` picks a smaller block
for a small device, and ToyOS's block devices have 4096-byte blocks. 32 MiB is
the smallest volume that formats, fills and passes fsck at that block size: 16
and 24 MiB both fail during format, and 8 and 12 MiB fail even at the default
block size. `bcachefs show-super` on the result:

    block_size:                              4.00k

`bcachefs fsck -y` on the committed volume, every pass:

    check_snapshot_trees... done (0 seconds)
    check_snapshots... done (0 seconds)
    check_subvols... done (0 seconds)
    check_subvol_children... done (0 seconds)
    check_inodes... done (0 seconds)
    check_extents... done (0 seconds)
    check_indirect_extents... done (0 seconds)
    check_dirents... done (0 seconds)
    check_xattrs... done (0 seconds)
    check_damage... done (0 seconds)
    check_root... done (0 seconds)
    check_unreachable_inodes... done (0 seconds)
    check_subvolume_structure... done (0 seconds)
    check_directory_structure... done (0 seconds)
    check_nlinks... done (0 seconds)
    check_reconcile_work... done (0 seconds)
    clean shutdown complete, journal seq 31

**The contents are this project's own test bytes.** Every file in the volume was
written by the commands above out of this repository's own material — an
eleven-byte string, the output of `seq`, an empty directory, a symlink — so
nothing third-party is stored here. bcachefs-tools is GPL-2.0, and that covers
the tool, not the bytes a user's own data becomes when the tool writes it: the
layout is a published on-disk format, not a copyrightable work of the program,
and the file contents are ours. The tool is not distributed with this repository
in any form.

`encrypted.img.gz` and `encrypted-wide-macs.img.gz` are encrypted volumes
upstream's tools wrote, which the engine unlocks and reads: ChaCha20/Poly1305
with 80-bit data MACs, and with the 128-bit ones that `--wide_macs` sets. They were produced by `bcachefs-tools` v1.39.4 (commit
d997ad76e906), built from its tag, in a QEMU guest booted from the Ubuntu
26.04.1 arm64 cloud image, as a development instrument on the same terms as
above. The commands, against a raw image file, the second time with
`--wide_macs` and the label `toyosencw`:

    truncate -s 32M enc.img
    printf '%s\n' 'toyos-test-passphrase' > pass
    bcachefs format --block_size=4096 --encrypted --passphrase_file pass --fs_label=toyosenc enc.img
    bcachefs unlock -k session -f pass enc.img      # in a fresh session keyring
    bcachefs fusemount enc.img /mnt/t
    printf hello-toyos > /mnt/t/a.txt; seq 1 40000 > /mnt/t/seq.txt; sync
    fusermount3 -u /mnt/t
    bcachefs fsck -n -K enc.img                     # exit 0
    gzip -9n < enc.img > enc.img.gz

The passphrase is a published test value. The files are an eleven-byte string,
the output of `seq` and an empty `lost+found`; the UUIDs, hash seeds and keys
inside are generated values; the member's device name is the image file's
basename, and no model or serial is recorded. Timestamps are 2026-10-22, the
guest's clock. The same reasoning as for `crc32c.img.gz` makes the bytes this
project's own.

`links.img.gz` is a volume upstream's tools populated with hard links and a
multi-level tree, against which the engine's derivation of link counts,
allocation, backpointers and accounting is compared. It was produced by the
same tools in the same guest, on the same terms. The commands, against a raw
image file:

    truncate -s 32M links.img
    bcachefs format --block_size=4096 --fs_label=toyoslinks links.img
    bcachefs fusemount links.img /mnt/t
    cd /mnt/t
    mkdir -p a/b/c/d/e empty
    printf hello-toyos > a/hello.txt
    ln a/hello.txt a/b/hello-link
    ln a/hello.txt a/b/c/d/e/hello-deep
    seq 1 40000 > a/b/seq.txt
    ln a/b/seq.txt seq-again
    ln -s ../hello.txt a/b/link
    : > a/b/c/empty-file
    mkdir many; for i in $(seq 1 600); do echo $i > many/f$i; done
    ln many/f1 many/f1-twin
    sync; cd /
    fusermount3 -u /mnt/t        # then wait until the FUSE daemon exits,
                                 # which writes the clean superblock
    bcachefs fsck -n -K links.img < /dev/null    # exit 0
    gzip -9n < links.img > links.img.gz

The files are an eleven-byte string, the output of `seq`, an empty file and
directory, a symlink, the numbers 1 to 600 and an empty `lost+found`; the
UUIDs and hash seeds inside are generated values; the member's device name is
the image file's basename, and no model or serial is recorded. Timestamps are
2026-10-23, the guest's clock. The same reasoning as for `crc32c.img.gz` makes
the bytes this project's own.

`unclean.img.gz` is a volume upstream's tools left mid-write, which the
engine reads by replaying its journal. It was produced by the same tools, in
the guest that made the encrypted fixtures, on the same terms. The commands,
against a raw image file:

    truncate -s 32M dirty.img
    bcachefs format --block_size=4096 dirty.img
    bcachefs fusemount dirty.img /mnt/t
    for i in $(seq 1 200); do echo $i > /mnt/t/f$i; done; sync
    kill -STOP <the FUSE daemon>     # stopped, so the copy is atomic
    cp --sparse=always dirty.img dirty-crash.img
    kill -CONT <the FUSE daemon>
    bcachefs fsck -n -K dirty-crash.img          # exit 0: replays 61-62
    gzip -9n < dirty-crash.img > dirty-crash.img.gz

The files are the numbers 1 to 200 and an empty `lost+found`; the UUIDs and
hash seeds inside are generated values; the member's device name is the image
file's basename, and no model or serial is recorded. Timestamps are
2026-10-22, the guest's clock. The same reasoning as for `crc32c.img.gz` makes
the bytes this project's own.

`unclean-32k-nodes.img.gz` is the same, made by the same commands but on a
64 MiB image, so its btree nodes are 32 KiB: the engine writes no volume of
smaller nodes. It was made by the same tools in the judge's Ubuntu guest, on
the same terms; its `fsck -n -K` exits 0, replaying 18-18 past unflushed
entries 19-22. Timestamps are 2026-10-24, the guest's clock.


